@*
    The four answers to "a file has arrived and you already have empires".

    One row, everywhere the question is asked - importing from disk, connecting to a file at a
    provider, and coming back to one that moved. The question is the same each time, so the answers
    are too: somebody who learns this row once should never meet a different version of it.

    They run in increasing order of consequence, which is the convention every other dialog in this
    app follows, and here that order is also the whole meaning. Left to right: keep all of mine,
    keep both favouring mine, keep both favouring theirs, take all of theirs. The two in the middle
    carry an arrow rather than a sentence, because the row already says which side is which - the
    arrow points at the end of the row whose copy survives.

    There is no cancel among them. For an import and for a file that moved underneath, "Keep
    Current" is what cancel would do, and offering both would be two buttons for one answer. Where
    backing out is genuinely a different thing - connecting, where keeping your own still points
    Save at the file - it is the dialog's own dismissal: Escape, or the backdrop.
*@

<button type="button" title="@KeepHint" aria-label="@KeepHint"
        @onclick="() => Chose(Arrival.KeepMine)">
    Keep Current
</button>

<button type="button" title="@MineHint" aria-label="@MineHint"
        @onclick="() => Chose(Arrival.MineWin)">
    <span aria-hidden="true">&#8592;</span> Merge
</button>

<button type="button" title="@TheirsHint" aria-label="@TheirsHint"
        @onclick="() => Chose(Arrival.TheirsWin)">
    Merge <span aria-hidden="true">&#8594;</span>
</button>

@*
    The only one that loses something, so the only one marked as such - and last, so it is never
    where a harmless answer was a moment ago.
*@
<button type="button" class="danger" title="@TakeHint" aria-label="@TakeHint"
        @onclick="() => Chose(Arrival.TakeTheirs)">
    @Take
</button>

@code {
    /// <summary>What to call the arriving file in the tooltips: "the file", "OneDrive".</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Short enough to sit inside a sentence. The whole name or path belongs in the question above,
    /// where there is room for it.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required string Theirs { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The last button's words: "Take File", "Take Cloud".</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required string Take { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Called with whichever answer was pressed.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required EventCallback<Arrival> OnChosen { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What each answer does, in a sentence.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// The arrows are quick to read and impossible to be sure of, which is exactly what a tooltip
    /// is for. They are the accessible name as well, since an arrow has none of its own.
    /// </remarks>
    private string KeepHint =>
        $"Keep the empires you have open now, and leave {Theirs} as it is.";

    private string MineHint =>
        $"Keep both. Where the same empire is in both, the one you have open is the one kept.";

    private string TheirsHint =>
        $"Keep both. Where the same empire is in both, the copy in {Theirs} is the one kept.";

    private string TakeHint =>
        $"Replace the empires you have open with the ones in {Theirs}.";

    private Task Chose(Arrival choice) => OnChosen.InvokeAsync(choice);
}
